# Step 1: Verify Node.js Installation

Before creating a TypeScript project, make sure Node.js is installed.

Open your terminal and run:

```bash
node -v
npm -v
```

Expected output:

```bash
v22.x.x
10.x.x
```

If Node.js is not installed, download it from:

[Node.js Official Website](https://nodejs.org?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

---

# Step 2: Create a New Project Folder

Create a folder for your TypeScript learning project.

```bash
mkdir typescript-learning
cd typescript-learning
```

Verify current directory:

```bash
pwd
```

---

# Step 3: Initialize a Node.js Project

Generate a `package.json` file.

```bash
npm init -y
```

This creates:

```text
typescript-learning
└── package.json
```

Example:

```json
{
  "name": "typescript-learning",
  "version": "1.0.0"
}
```

---

# Step 4: Install TypeScript

Install TypeScript as a development dependency.

```bash
npm install --save-dev typescript
```

Verify installation:

```bash
npx tsc --version
```

Example output:

```bash
Version 5.x.x
```

---

# Step 5: Generate TypeScript Configuration

Create a `tsconfig.json` file.

```bash
npx tsc --init
```

Project structure:

```text
typescript-learning
├── node_modules
├── package.json
└── tsconfig.json
```

---

# Step 6: Configure tsconfig.json

Open `tsconfig.json` and replace its contents with:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "CommonJS",
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "outDir": "./dist",
    "strict": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  }
}
```

### Explanation

| Option          | Purpose                      |
| --------------- | ---------------------------- |
| target          | JavaScript version generated |
| module          | Module system used           |
| rootDir         | TypeScript source folder     |
| outDir          | Compiled JavaScript folder   |
| strict          | Enables strict type checking |
| esModuleInterop | Better CommonJS support      |
| skipLibCheck    | Faster compilation           |

---

# Step 7: Create Source Folder

Create a source directory.

```bash
mkdir src
```

Project structure:

```text
typescript-learning
├── src
├── node_modules
├── package.json
└── tsconfig.json
```

---

# Step 8: Create Your First TypeScript File

Create:

```bash
touch src/index.ts
```

Add:

```typescript
const message: string = "Hello TypeScript";

console.log(message);
```

---

# Step 9: Compile TypeScript

Run:

```bash
npx tsc
```

This creates:

```text
dist/
└── index.js
```

Generated JavaScript:

```javascript
const message = "Hello TypeScript";
console.log(message);
```

---

# Step 10: Execute the Generated JavaScript

Run:

```bash
node dist/index.js
```

Output:

```bash
Hello TypeScript
```

---

# Step 11: Add Build Script

Open `package.json`.

Replace scripts section:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsc"
  }
}
```

Now compile using:

```bash
npm run build
```

---

# Step 12: Add Start Script

Update scripts:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsc",
    "start": "node dist/index.js"
  }
}
```

Run:

```bash
npm start
```

---

# Step 13: Install ts-node (Optional but Recommended)

Normally:

```text
TypeScript -> Compile -> JavaScript -> Run
```

With `ts-node`:

```text
TypeScript -> Run Directly
```

Install:

```bash
npm install --save-dev ts-node
```

Run:

```bash
npx ts-node src/index.ts
```

Output:

```bash
Hello TypeScript
```

---

# Step 14: Add Development Script

Update scripts:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsc",
    "start": "node dist/index.js",
    "dev": "ts-node src/index.ts"
  }
}
```

Run:

```bash
npm run dev
```

---

# Step 15: Install Auto-Restart Development Tool

Install:

```bash
npm install --save-dev nodemon
```

Update scripts:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsc",
    "start": "node dist/index.js",
    "dev": "nodemon --exec ts-node src/index.ts"
  }
}
```

Now run:

```bash
npm run dev
```

Whenever you save a `.ts` file, the application restarts automatically.

---

# Step 16: Recommended Final Folder Structure

```text
typescript-learning/
│
├── src/
│   ├── index.ts
│   ├── basics/
│   ├── interfaces/
│   ├── classes/
│   ├── generics/
│   ├── enums/
│   └── advanced/
│
├── dist/
│
├── node_modules/
│
├── package.json
├── package-lock.json
└── tsconfig.json
```

---

# Step 17: Create a Learning Roadmap Inside This Project

As you learn, create one file per topic:

```text
src/
├── 01-variables.ts
├── 02-functions.ts
├── 03-arrays.ts
├── 04-objects.ts
├── 05-unions.ts
├── 06-interfaces.ts
├── 07-types.ts
├── 08-enums.ts
├── 09-generics.ts
├── 10-classes.ts
├── 11-inheritance.ts
├── 12-utility-types.ts
├── 13-decorators.ts
├── 14-modules.ts
└── 15-advanced-types.ts
```

This approach turns the project into a complete TypeScript playground where you can experiment with every feature independently.

---

# Final Command Summary

```bash
mkdir typescript-learning

cd typescript-learning

npm init -y

npm install --save-dev typescript

npx tsc --init

mkdir src

touch src/index.ts

npm install --save-dev ts-node nodemon

npm run dev
```

Since you're already a JavaScript trainer, I would recommend learning TypeScript in this order:

1. Type Annotations
2. Type Inference
3. Union Types
4. Interfaces
5. Type Aliases
6. Generics
7. Utility Types
8. Classes
9. Advanced Types
10. Declaration Files (`.d.ts`)
11. TypeScript Compiler Internals
12. Building a small Node.js + Express API in TypeScript
13. Building a React application with TypeScript
14. Building a NestJS application with TypeScript

This sequence takes you from "JavaScript developer" to "production-grade TypeScript developer" with a solid understanding of how TypeScript works under the hood.
